    "summary": "Kente cloth, a rectangular man’s wrapper composed of 20 hand-woven narrow strips of imported European cotton, hand-sewn together along the selvedges. The strips are designed and assembled alternately to form a chequerboard pattern along the entire cloth; there is no variant pattern at the ends. All the strips have a uniform warp design: this consists primarily of red cotton, along which run four stripes of black, yellow and white. The weft pattern is formed of pairs of blocks yellow and blue supplementary weft between which are woven discontinuous lines of geometrical and zig-zag designs. The ends have been hemmed.",
